  t("<b>Conservatism</b> (Munro school)", "<b>Thomas Munro</b>, Governor of Madras <b>1820-27</b>: govern through Indian traditions, but British govern, no leadership for Indians", "<b>Ryotwari (1801-07, 1820+) in Madras</b>: State collects from peasant directly (ancient model), but all by EIC officials", "<b>Orientalist in form, Authoritarian in substance</b>", "Same school: <b>Elphinstone, Malcolm, Metcalfe</b>; Elphinstone (Munro's disciple) brought Ryotwari to Bombay", w=22),
  sec("1813 TURN: LIBERALISM, UTILITARIANISM, EVANGELICALISM", 'b'),
  t("<b>Why the turn in 1813</b>", "<b>1</b> EIC beat all major powers (only <b>Holkar, Bhonsle, Sikh State</b> left) <b>2</b> huge army <b>3</b> Napoleon beaten <b>4</b> Industrial Revolution in full swing", "Confident to rule 'the British way: openly, aggressively, without apology'", "<b>New economic motive:</b> western tastes and education = a market for British goods", w=22),
  t("<b>Liberalism</b>", "Orientalism governed badly: <b>Great Bengal Famine 1770</b>, one crore died while the EIC doubled land revenue", "Modernise with the best ideas (best = west); free India from <b>feudal polity, economy (agrarian, closed, unequal), society (birth discrimination, no women's rights)</b>", t("<b>Thomas Macaulay</b> (Law Member, Charter Act 1833)", "<b>Law Commission 1834</b>: western-style laws codified by 1837", "<b>Minute on Education 1835</b>: English-medium western education, <b>Brown Sahibs</b> (brown outside, British inside) = cultural intermediaries", "<b>Charter Act 1813</b> (Liberal-influenced): ended EIC trade monopoly, non-EIC British business enters = economic liberalism for British, not Indian, business"), "<b>Bentinck 28-35</b>: <b>Sati Abolition Act 1829</b> (Ch 19)", w=26),
